I’ve resumed from the retained three-phase refinement. The established assignments are monoclinic WO₃, scheelite CaWO₄, and calcite; I’ll now check the interrupted physically bounded refinement, complete the final GSAS-II model, and export/validate every required artifact from that same project.

The bounded continuation completed successfully and slightly improved the fit to Rwp 7.02%. It still has several parameters pinned at imposed limits, so I’m checking whether those boundaries reflect the data or merely transfer correlations among profile, displacement, and displacement-factor terms before freezing the submitted model.

The current residual is dominated by peak-shape/intensity mismatch, not an unmodeled background or extra peaks. I’m now testing the discrete specimen models actually allowed by the measurement description (strain axes, texture axes, and optional roughness) while keeping profile parameters physically bounded; this also avoids submitting the interrupted project’s stale, non-converged boundary state.

The grid is still refining; it is evaluating 20 common-profile decompositions against the same three-phase model. I’ll use the best converged interior solution only as a baseline, then test phase-specific strain/texture choices one at a time so the final phase fractions are not selected from an unstable boundary fit.

The scan is slower than expected because each variant is being refined twice and rechecked after any bound is reached. It is making progress in the retained log; I’m letting this one complete because it directly resolves the strongest width/fraction correlation in the current model.

This pass is computationally heavy but bounded: 40 total least-squares passes. The final choice will still require convergence and physical-screen checks, so a numerically lower Rwp alone will not be accepted.

The all-coordinate WO₃ trial lowered Rwp by only 0.06 percentage point but drove several W–O distances to 1.60 and 2.38 Å and moved oxygen sites by far more than the pattern can support. I’m rejecting that overfit. The defensible model keeps the published oxygen framework, refines the well-scattering W sites and calcite’s carbonate coordinate, and now removes the remaining asymmetry/zero correlation to obtain a clean final convergence.

The restrained scheelite-coordinate trial converged at Rwp 7.06% and kept tetrahedral W–O at 1.843 Å; the unconstrained value was 1.965 Å and was rejected. I’m using this restrained, converged project for the deliverables, with the minor-phase oxygen position explicitly marked as model-dependent. Next I’m exporting all artifacts from that exact project and validating CIF chemistry, geometry, fraction normalization, and profile replay consistency.

